Hi everyone,

Quick note about yesterday morning. This was not a drill.

What happened: an invoice-themed phishing email landed in a bunch of
inboxes, dressed up to look like it came from our expense tool. It was
sent from lanternfell-expense-invoices.example -- a lookalike domain that
has nothing to do with us. Worth saying plainly: the real Lanternfell
Expense never emails you from that domain, and it never asks you to sign
in from a link in an invoice notice.

The good news, and the reason I'm writing: you handled it exactly right.
At least eight of you reported it within minutes using the report button,
two also opened Torchstone Desk tickets, and that gave us what we needed to
block the sender fast. No accounts were compromised. Nothing to clean up.
That's the system working the way it's supposed to.

Three tells this one had, so you can spot the next one:

- Urgency: "action required," "invoice overdue," a deadline meant to make
  you click before you think.
- A lookalike domain: close to a real name but not quite -- always check
  the full sender address, not just the display name.
- A sign-in prompt: a link that wants your credentials on a page you didn't
  navigate to yourself.

What to do when something looks off:

- Use the report button in your mail client. That's the fastest path and it
  loops us in automatically.
- Not sure? Ask in #security on Glimmerfen Chat before you click. There is
  no dumb question here.
- Reporting is never punished. Ever. We would rather chase ten false alarms
  than miss one real lure. If you clicked before you reported, tell us
  anyway -- you're not in trouble, we just want to check.

One heads-up: our next phishing simulation campaign is coming in June.
February's numbers were 14% clicked and 22% reported, and yesterday shows
we can do a lot better than that. Let's beat both.

Thanks again for the fast reporting. This is exactly the muscle we want.

